cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Starboard — the specification, the reference implementations,
  the conformance suite or the evidence — please cite it as below.
type: software
title: "Starboard: an open, deterministic give-way protocol for satellite collision avoidance"
abstract: >-
  Starboard is an open "rules of the road" for satellites: a deterministic
  collision-avoidance and give-way protocol in which two spacecraft from rival
  operators, with no radio contact, independently compute the identical
  verdict — down to the last bit — about which one maneuvers. It ingests
  standard CCSDS Conjunction Data Messages (CDMs), computes a covariance-aware
  probability of collision (Patera contour integral), applies five
  COLREGS-inspired rules (capability, tier, overtaking, starboard, fair coin)
  on a canonical pair ordering, and plans a minimum-delta-v Clohessy-Wiltshire
  burn that is exactly reversible back to the slot. A flock layer spreads
  maneuver notices hop by hop so a whole constellation shares one picture of
  who is moving. The repository provides a C++17 reference, an independent
  MISRA-checked C99 flight library (43 KB, no heap, no recursion) with
  built-in self-test, SEU-tolerant redundant evaluation and CCSDS telemetry,
  and the evidence: bit-identical transcripts across compilers, languages and
  11 CPU architectures (x86, ARM, RAD750-class PowerPC, RISC-V, SPARC, MIPS,
  s390x) and bare-metal Cortex-M microcontrollers, CBMC proofs of the
  agreement theorem, Wycheproof-validated signature verification, and
  fuzzing.
authors:
